      "student_name": "Meera Nair",
      "subject": "Physics",
      "question": "How do I use Ohm's law when a circuit has two resistors in series? I can calculate one resistor, but I mix up the total voltage.",
      "status": "Resolved",
      "created_at": "2026-11-02T08:30:00",
      "answers": [
        {
          "id": "a1",
          "author_id": "u1",
          "author_name": "Rithvi",
          "explanation": "Add the resistors first: R_total = R1 + R2. The same current flows through both. Then V_total = I \u00d7 R_total. The voltage across each resistor is I \u00d7 R for that resistor, and those two voltages add back to the battery voltage.",
          "points_awarded": 25,
          "verified": true,
          "upvotes": [],
          "author_username": "rithvi_11"
        }
      ],
      "student_username": "meera.n1"
    },
    {
